  "text": {
    "puzzle": "puzzle",
    "card": "Play a take-away game on three numbered piles, then return the same counters.",
    "nim": "Nim practice table",
    "piles": "three-pile take-away game",
    "hands_empty": "Put down what you hold before changing a pile.",
    "insufficient": "That pile has fewer counters than this removal needs. Choose an available positive amount from one pile.",
    "rules": "This normal-play Nim practice table starts with piles A, B and C containing 3, 4 and 5 integral depicted counters. Seen from the front contact side, A is the far row, B the middle and C the near row. The blue strip in each row is active; the brown strip is spent. Fixed numbers increase left to right. Each original counter always stays in its own numbered column, either active or spent. If a pile has n remaining, positions 1 through n are active. Removing k changes exactly that pile by k and indexes its highest remaining numbered counters to their spent positions. Other piles do not change.",
    "game": "For normal play, agree who starts and alternate by agreement. On each move remove any positive available number from exactly one pile. The person taking the final counter wins by the stated game rule. Forms enforce only the chosen pile amount: they do not assign players, enforce turns, declare a winner, provide strategy or act as an opponent. The worked 3/4/5 example is A2, C3, B1, B1, A1, B1, C1, B1, C1; the final piles are empty. Return-all restores the same twelve depicted counters to their active positions and the counts to 3/4/5. This fixed indexed practice drawing has no loose inventory, usable-resource creation, custody, physical counter-transfer path or exact individual-token contact claim. Each removal commits one whole negative count adjustment at native hand contact; there is no solver, timer or score."
  },
